Build command g++ main.cpp -o test -I./ -L./build -lsyncsdk  -lpthread

Run command LD_LIBRARY_PATH=./build ./test
Main functions
    Automatic device enumeration and format configuration
    Scan all USB cameras, set resolution and frame rate, and identify the master device.
    The fixed format can be changed by modifying `int FormatIndex = 0;`. If the device does not support the default frame rate, the program will exit and prompt. The program will only start normally when the selected frame rate matches the hardware-supported frame rate.
Real-time sync frame detection
    When the exposure time difference between the minimum and maximum exposure devices is <= 100us, it is determined to be synchronized and output to the screen.
    For devices with the 5th digit of the device ID as (a, b, c), exposure start time is used for synchronization.
    For devices with the 5th digit of the device ID as (d, e, f), exposure end time is used for synchronization.
Dynamic terminal display
    Continuously refresh and display detailed information of each synced frame group on the terminal, including: 
	Device index, imageFormat, resolution, data size, real-time FPS
	Exposure start/end time, exposure duration
	IMU data associated with each device (acceleration, gyroscope, timestamp)
Keyboard save
    Press the Space key to save the synced frame group shown on the screen. It automatically creates a subfolder named with the current system time (YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZZZ), containing: 
	YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZZZ.jpg - Stitched multi-channel NV12 images (converted to JPG)
	YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZZZ.txt - Text file containing frame metadata, IMU table, and global exposure statistics for each channel

Hardware requirements
    At least 2 synchronized cameras(Support hardware trigger sync), One device must have a hardware PID (extracted from VID:PID) with the last two decimal digits as 01, which will act as the master device.

The program continuously refreshes the terminal (clears and redraws) to display information about the latest synced frame group. Example:
Camera 0: MJPG 1920x1080, size=123456 bytes, fps=30.00
exposure: start=  1234567890 us end=  1234568890 us, duration=    1000 us
[g] 0x0 Acc=(  0.123456, -0.234567,  9.810000) Gyro=(  0.001234,  0.002345, -0.003456) time=  1234567900 us
[g] 0x1 Acc=(  0.123456, -0.234567,  9.810000) Gyro=(  0.001234,  0.002345, -0.003456) time=  1234567920 us
...

Camera 1: MJPG 1920x1080, size=123456 bytes, fps=30.00
exposure: start=  1234567895 us end=  1234568895 us, duration=    1000 us
[g] 0x0 Acc=(  0.123456, -0.234567,  9.810000) Gyro=(  0.001234,  0.002345, -0.003456) time=  1234567905 us
[g] 0x1 Acc=(  0.123456, -0.234567,  9.810000) Gyro=(  0.001234,  0.002345, -0.003456) time=  1234567925 us
...
Camera X: Logical device index
Format, resolution, data size, real-time FPS
exposure: Exposure start time, Exposure end time, Exposure duration (microseconds)
IMU line: IMU sample index, Acceleration (X/Y/Z), Gyroscope (X/Y/Z), Timestamp
 
